Before any main work, please do a read-only inspection pass and report back — do not write the full solution or run long simulations yet.

Specifically, I need:
1. Contents (verbatim, or a faithful structured dump) of `./data/task_input.json`, especially the exact `protocol` block: `lambda_scan`, `beta_fit_lambdas`, `system_sizes`, `n_seeds`, `t_max`, `seed`, plus the model/spec fields (site states, absorbing state, birth/death rules, parity modulation, left/right asymmetry, update scheme). Confirm there are no extra fields I should be aware of, and confirm which fields are absent (no invented aliases).
2. A listing of the current workspace (does `./results/` exist, is there a pre-existing `analysis.py` or harness, available packages/versions, any CPU/runtime hints).
3. A proposed approach/plan covering:
   - the simulation kernel you intend to implement (random-sequential update as `L` random site visits per sweep, active-site death probability `dt`, parity/asymmetric activation probability for inactive sites, periodic boundaries, fully active initial condition) and how you will keep it exactly as input-defined rather than an alias/approximation;
   - how you will estimate `lambda_c` from finite data, given the brief's warning to not rely solely on the largest-system decay curve, and to check whether the smallest system lands in an intermediate survival regime rather than being nearly always dead or alive (state the specific criterion, e.g. band on survival probability / finite-size crossing of scaled quantities, plus the CI construction);
   - the `beta` fit: which `beta_fit_lambdas` and `system_sizes` points you will use, the functional form, the late-time `rho_ss` averaging rule (single consistent rule across scan points), and how you will avoid `beta` claims hinging on one large size;
   - the dynamic-scaling / `z` treatment: how you will attempt collapse, and explicit criteria for declaring dynamic scaling "weakly constrained" and preferring `inconclusive` over overclaiming `z` (including how `z_q_used`, `tau_q_half`, `collapse_success`, `universality_supported`, `static_evidence_consistent`, `dynamic_scale_observed` in `dp_report.json` will be populated);
   - the exact columns/keys for the four required outputs and the runtime/time budget you expect at the prescribed `n_seeds`/`t_max`, plus any vectorisation/batching strategy and how you will report reproducibility from `seed`.
4. A short list of consequential assumptions or ambiguities you want me to weigh in on (e.g. definition of `survival_prob` vs `rho`, time origin for `rho(t)`, how `lambda` multiplies the activation strength, whether `beta` is fit on `rho_ss` vs `rho(t)` at a fixed late window, CI method and confidence level).

Keep it to inspection + plan + assumptions; no results files yet.

Please do NOT produce the full analysis yet. For this first turn, do only the following and report back:

1. Inspection of the five inputs in `data/`:
   - `control_primary.json`: list every key and value verbatim (setting_key, lattice size list, temperature grid, any seeds/beta/units metadata).
   - `scan_axis_a.npy` and `scan_axis_b.npy`: report length, dtype, min/max, monotonicity, and your inferred physical role (which is temperature `T`, which is lattice size `L`) with the numerical reason you are inferring it from.
   - `probe_axis_a.npy`: length, range, spacing type (linear or log), inferred role (radial distance bins for correlation profiles?).
   - `state_tokens.npz`: report array names, shapes, dtypes, and a short interpretation of the state bank (are these stored spin/vortex configurations, or precomputed observables, or token/label arrays?). If configurations are stored, say what lattice geometry and boundary convention they imply.

2. A proposed approach, as a short ordered plan, covering:
   - how the model/Hamiltonian will be reconstructed if only a state bank is provided (explicitly state whether the state bank is sufficient to compute `Upsilon`, defect density, `xi_2nd/L`, `chi`, and radial profiles, or whether you must re-simulate from a recovered model definition);
   - which estimator definitions you will use for helicity modulus (energy-difference/winding formulation), defect density, second-moment correlation length, susceptibility, and the exponent `eta(T)`;
   - how the BKT-style fit routes in `phase_fit_diagnostics.json` will be implemented: Weber-Minnhagen stiffness fit with fitted offset `C`, susceptibility log-correction fit `A*L^(7/4)*(log(L)+C)^(1/8)`, the `x = 1/log(L)` intercept routes in the stated component order, the residual/response sweeps over `L_min` with >=3 sizes, jackknife blocks, and how the single `preferred_transition_temperature` is chosen;
   - how the profile selection (`selected_profile_indices`, `obs_tensor_c`, `profile_fit_window`) will be tied to the transition estimate.

3. Explicitly flag assumptions and open uncertainties, in particular:
   - whether the units of `scan_axis_a` are `T` or `beta` (i.e. whether `Upsilon/T` and `2*T/pi` need beta-conversion);
   - the random-number / thermalisation policy and number of samples, and whether that budget is realistic on CPU for the largest lattice;
   - the definition of `eta(T)` at the largest lattice and the window used to extract it from `obs_tensor_c`;
   - any ambiguity in `stiffness_jump_ratio_at_transition`, `dimensionless_ratio_at_transition`, or `transition_estimate_spread` that is not fully pinned down by the brief.

Keep it concrete and numbers-first. Do not write `simulation.py` or any output artifact in this turn.

Hola! Before any coding, please do ONLY inspection and planning — no full solution yet.

1) Inspect `./data/task_input.json` and report: number of cases; for each case_id the values of `nx, ny, uin, re, square_center, square_size, n_steps, warmup_steps, sample_every`; plus your derived `nu = uin*square_size/re` and `tau = 0.5 + 3*nu`, and the implied solid mask bounds from `cx, cy, half = square_size // 2`. Confirm every case has >= 10 sampleable steps under `(step - warmup_steps) % sample_every == 0` within `n_steps`, and flag any case where the square would clip the domain or where `tau` is dangerously close to 0.5 (low Re / stability risk).

2) Propose your approach for the Lattice Boltzmann part only (this must be a real LBM solver, not a fitted profile or formula shortcut):
- lattice/scheme (e.g. D2Q9 BGK) and lattice-Bouchut/bounce-back-on-solid treatment for the square no-slip region;
- inflow/outflow and wall boundary plan (e.g. Zou-He velocity inlet, outlet extrapolation or fixed distribution), and the deterministic initial field (uniform `uin`, `f = f_eq`) since the contract forbids hidden pre-settling or extra warmup;
- force evaluation: exactly which momentum-exchange quantities you accumulate per solid cell per step to get `Fx, Fy`, and confirmation that `cd = Fx/(0.5*uin**2*square_size)` and `cl = Fy/(0.5*uin**2*square_size)`;
- how the centerline profile (`nx` rows, all `x`) and the transverse profile at `x_probe = min(nx-1, cx + 2*square_size)` (`ny` rows, `u,v`) are extracted from the simulated field;
- runtime plan: pure numpy vectorized vs optional numba `@njit`, expected wall-clock for the largest case, and how you keep everything finite (no NaN/Inf) without adding relaxation hacks;
- `converged` definition you intend to write in `fluid_summary.csv` (I want a defensible, stated criterion, e.g. drift of windowed `cd_mean` or `cl_rms`, not a hardcoded `True`).

3) List consequential assumptions/uncertainties only, e.g. whether `square_center` is the lower-left-anchored anchor implied by the mask formula, definition of `converged`, ordering of CSV rows, and whether `fluid_report.json`'s `units` and `cd_mean_avg` should be lattice units and an unweighted mean across cases.

Return inspection facts + approach + assumptions. Do not write `analysis.py` or any `results/` artifact this turn.

Buenos días. Antes de escribir una sola línea de simulación, le pido SOLO tres cosas (sin código completo todavía):

(1) Inspección de entradas. Cargue `data/initial_u_x.npy` y `data/initial_u_y.npy` e informe: shapes reales y dtype, si son efectivamente C/F-contiguous, y estadísticos mínimos (min, max, media, norma L2, media de 0.5·dx²·(ū²+v̄²) con dx=1/135) para validar que el campo inicial es de hecho el par de anillos vorticales del "leapfrog" clásico (dos pares contra-rotantes). Indique también si los máximos de |u| son compatibles con CFL=1.8797 en una malla 135×135, es decir dt ≈ 1.8797·dx/max|u|, y cuántos pasos internos por frame eso implicaría aproximadamente. Confirme que no hay NaN/Inf.

(2) Propuesta de método, en prosa estructurada (pseudocódigo de nivel alto, no la solución final). Necesito que declare explícitamente:
- convención de la malla MAC escalonada: qué indexación usa ψ (¿nodos en centros de celda, 135×135?), y cómo reconstruye u_x[i,j]=(ψ[i,j+1]−ψ[i,j])/dx y u_y[i,j]=−(ψ[i+1,j]−ψ[i,j])/dx en caras (136×135 y 135×136);
- cómo cierra el stencil de Poisson de 5 puntos con Dirichlet ψ=0 en la frontera y qué solver concreto usará (p.ej. `scipy.sparse.linalg.spsolve` factorizado una sola vez, o un método iterativo con tol < 1e-12) y cómo verificará el residuo real ||Aψ+b||;
- orden exacto del ciclo por paso: G2P con kernel B-spline cuadrático N₂ (interpolación de ω y de ∇ω), T=I por paso, semi-Lagrangiano RK4 de ω_n hacia atrás 0.5·dt para u_mid, RK4 de partículas y dT/dt=−(∇u)ᵀ·T con derivadas dN₂, C_c=Tᵀ·init_C_c, P2G con corrección afín y normalización de pesos, Poisson, reconstrucción de velocidad, BC de velocidad normal nula, recomputo de ω nodal;
- cómo implementará el kernel N₂ sobre la malla escalonada y el clamp de posiciones a [dx/2, 1−dx/2]²;
- política de timestep: dt=min(CFL·dx/max|u|, tiempo restante al frame);
- coste computacional estimado en CPU para 107 frames × (pasos por frame), con 16 partículas/celda (~291 600 partículas), y en qué partes vectorizará con NumPy; si prevé que no cabe en un tiempo razonable, dígaselo ya y proponga la optimización (p.ej. dispersión/ gathering vectorizado por binning, o reutilizar la factorización de Poisson), no lo descubra a mitad de la corrida.

(3) Supuestos e incertidumbres que necesita aprobar conmigo, en particular: los frames guardados son [0,26,52,78,104] para vorticidad pero la energía se guarda en frames 0..107 (108 valores, incluyendo el frame inicial y los frames intermedios de frame-index, no de sub-paso); ω de salida se calcula a partir de velocidades en centros de celda promediadas desde las caras con diferencias centrales y clamp de índices fuera de rango (borde); dtype float32 al guardar; y que el campo inicial se interpreta directamente como velocidades en caras de la malla objetivo. Si su lectura del brief difiere en signo de ω (ω=∂v/∂x−∂u/∂y), en la normalización del P2G, o en si el Poisson resuelve ∇²ψ=−ω, máquelo ahora como decisión abierta.

No pida todavía el archivo `simulation.py` ejecutado: primero quiero ver la inspección, el plan y las banderas abiertas.

